WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.600
Have you ever sat down to take a test and&nbsp;
completely forgotten what you had studied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.720
or opened your fridge and not&nbsp;
remembered what you came to get?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.560
Quite often, we only remember these things once&nbsp;
we return to the place where we studied, or to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:25.120
where we had the initial craving for that snack.&nbsp;
These experiences are so common that psychologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.480
have studied them for years, and attribute them&nbsp;
to something called “context-dependent memory.”

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:37.040
What is context-dependent memory, and how does&nbsp;
it work? To understand, let’s first have a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:44.640
at how memories are formed. This usually happens&nbsp;
in three stages, encoding, storage, and retrieval.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.080
When you learn something new or have a new&nbsp;
experience -- say, you meet someone at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.440
party -- your brain takes this information&nbsp;
-- the person’s face, the sound of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.080
voice -- and transforms it into a memory code, a&nbsp;
pattern of activity in a network of nerve cells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.520
This is known as encoding. The encoded memories&nbsp;
are temporarily stored in short-term memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.640
and if you call upon them enough, they can&nbsp;
get transferred to more permanent storage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.360
called long-term memory, from where they can&nbsp;
be recalled whenever you need to use them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
This process of accessing information from&nbsp;
long-term memory is known as retrieval.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.680
The interesting thing about forming memories&nbsp;
is that your brain encodes and stores not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.520
the information that you are actively focused on&nbsp;
but also some of the contextual information from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.200
your surroundings. Things you might overlook&nbsp;
-- like the music playing in the background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.120
a subtle aroma in the room, or the physical layout&nbsp;
of the space -- are encoded in addition to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.920
new friend’s face and name. This link, between&nbsp;
the context and the thing you want to remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.760
is the basis for context-dependent memory. When&nbsp;
you find yourself struggling to remember the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.720
name of your dance partner, listening to&nbsp;
the song that you danced to or revisiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.320
the location of the party might help you&nbsp;
remember. If the context during retrieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.920
is similar to the context during encoding, you&nbsp;
are much more likely to recall the memory.
&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.280
The idea of context-dependent memory is not&nbsp;
only intuitive, it has also been scientifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:32.000
supported with experimental data. In one of the&nbsp;
classic studies on context-dependent memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.080
researchers asked two groups of divers to memorize&nbsp;
a list of words in two different contexts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.160
One group memorized the words&nbsp;
while they were underwater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.040
and the other group did this on land.&nbsp;
After the words had been memorized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.320
the researchers asked half the divers to recall&nbsp;
the words in their original learning context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.360
and the other half to recall the words in the&nbsp;
context where they did not learn the words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.800
The researchers found that words learned&nbsp;
underwater were best recalled underwater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:08.400
and vice versa. Other studies have&nbsp;
also shown that internal states and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.480
moods -- such as the feeling of hunger, happiness&nbsp;
or being caffeinated at the time of memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.720
encoding -- can help in the contextualization&nbsp;
of memory and operate as the retrieval cue.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.960
So what affects the strength of a&nbsp;
context-dependent memory? Some of the factors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:33.440
that enhance context-dependent memory at the time&nbsp;
of encoding are the richness of the context -- if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:40.160
a high number of cues stimulate many different&nbsp;
senses, the memory will be better contextualized;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.080
the uniqueness of the context -- say the high&nbsp;
volume of the music playing; and the level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.240
of attention you pay to small details in your&nbsp;
surroundings. Among factors that interfere with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:58.160
forming a strong context-dependent memory are high&nbsp;
stress levels or fatigue at the time of encoding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.560
since these things decrease your&nbsp;
attentiveness to contextual details.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:09.920
Now that you know how a specific smell, sound, or&nbsp;
feeling can influence the way you form and recall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:16.000
memories, you can try to use context-dependent&nbsp;
memory to your advantage. The next time you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:21.360
preparing for a test, try studying in a similar&nbsp;
environment to the one you will take your test in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.880
And if you forget what you wanted from the&nbsp;
fridge, try revisiting the place where you first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:41.840
thought about what snack you wanted. Most likely,&nbsp;
you’ll find the answers that you knew all along!

